### What is the problem?

Implement a FIFO queue using only LIFO stacks. A stack reverses order on transfer, so moving everything from one stack to another flips LIFO into FIFO. The challenge is doing it efficiently, not on every operation.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.util.ArrayDeque;
import java.util.Deque;

public class QueueFromStacks<T> {
    private final Deque<T> inbox = new ArrayDeque<>();
    private final Deque<T> outbox = new ArrayDeque<>();

    public void enqueue(T item) {
        inbox.push(item);
    }

    public T dequeue() {
        if (outbox.isEmpty()) {
            while (!inbox.isEmpty()) {
                outbox.push(inbox.pop());
            }
        }
        return outbox.pop();
    }

    public T peek() {
        if (outbox.isEmpty()) {
            while (!inbox.isEmpty()) {
                outbox.push(inbox.pop());
            }
        }
        return outbox.peek();
    }

    public boolean isEmpty() {
        return inbox.isEmpty() && outbox.isEmpty();
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        QueueFromStacks<String> q = new QueueFromStacks<>();
        q.enqueue("TXN-A");
        q.enqueue("TXN-B");
        q.enqueue("TXN-C");
        System.out.println(q.dequeue()); // TXN-A (FIFO)
        System.out.println(q.dequeue()); // TXN-B
        q.enqueue("TXN-D");
        System.out.println(q.dequeue()); // TXN-C
        System.out.println(q.dequeue()); // TXN-D
    }
}
```

```text
TXN-A
TXN-B
TXN-C
TXN-D
```

### Why does transferring between stacks reverse the order?

A stack is LIFO: the last element pushed is the first popped. When you pop from one stack and push to another, the bottom element of the first stack ends up on top of the second. That reversal turns LIFO into FIFO for the transferred batch.

```java
package com.bank.core;

import java.util.ArrayDeque;
import java.util.Deque;

public class ReversalDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Deque<String> inbox = new ArrayDeque<>();
        inbox.push("First");  // bottom
        inbox.push("Second");
        inbox.push("Third");  // top

        Deque<String> outbox = new ArrayDeque<>();
        while (!inbox.isEmpty()) {
            outbox.push(inbox.pop());
        }

        // Outbox now has First on top (reversed order)
        System.out.println(outbox.pop()); // First
        System.out.println(outbox.pop()); // Second
        System.out.println(outbox.pop()); // Third
    }
}
```

```text
First
Second
Third
```

### Why is lazy transfer important?

Transferring only when the outbox is empty is what gives amortized O(1). Each element moves from inbox to outbox exactly once in its lifetime: one push to inbox, one transfer pop and push to outbox, and one final pop from outbox. If you transferred eagerly on every dequeue, each element could move multiple times, destroying the amortized guarantee.

```java
package com.bank.core;

public class LazyTransferAnalysis {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // Element lifecycle:
        // 1. Push to inbox:      O(1)
        // 2. Transfer to outbox:  O(1) (happens once)
        // 3. Pop from outbox:    O(1)
        // Total per element:     O(3) = O(1) amortized

        System.out.println("Lazy transfer: each element moves at most once");
        System.out.println("Amortized cost per operation: O(1)");
        System.out.println("Eager transfer: element may move multiple times");
        System.out.println("Eager cost per dequeue: O(n) worst case every time");
    }
}
```

```text
Lazy transfer: each element moves at most once
Amortized cost per operation: O(1)
Eager transfer: element may move multiple times
Eager cost per dequeue: O(n) worst case every time
```

### How do you implement a stack using a queue?

With a single queue, make push the expensive operation. After adding the new element, rotate all earlier elements behind it by polling and re-offering them. This puts the newest element at the front, giving LIFO behavior on poll.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.util.ArrayDeque;
import java.util.Queue;

public class StackFromQueue<T> {
    private final Queue<T> queue = new ArrayDeque<>();

    public void push(T item) {
        int size = queue.size();
        queue.offer(item);
        for (int i = 0; i < size; i++) {
            queue.offer(queue.poll()); // rotate older elements behind
        }
    }

    public T pop() { return queue.poll(); }
    public T top() { return queue.peek(); }
    public boolean isEmpty() { return queue.isEmpty(); }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        StackFromQueue<String> s = new StackFromQueue<>();
        s.push("Bottom");
        s.push("Middle");
        s.push("Top");
        System.out.println(s.pop()); // Top (LIFO)
        System.out.println(s.pop()); // Middle
    }
}
```

```text
Top
Middle
```

### What are the complexity trade-offs?

For two-stack queue: enqueue is O(1), dequeue is amortized O(1) but worst-case O(n) for a single call that triggers a transfer. For single-queue stack: push is O(n) due to rotation, pop and top are O(1). The two-stack queue has the better amortized bound.

```java
package com.bank.core;

public class ComplexityComparison {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Queue from 2 stacks:");
        System.out.println("  enqueue: O(1)");
        System.out.println("  dequeue: amortized O(1), worst O(n)");
        System.out.println();
        System.out.println("Stack from 1 queue:");
        System.out.println("  push: O(n) (rotation)");
        System.out.println("  pop:  O(1)");
    }
}
```

```text
Queue from 2 stacks:
  enqueue: O(1)
  dequeue: amortized O(1), worst O(n)

Stack from 1 queue:
  push: O(n) (rotation)
  pop:  O(1)
```

### What are common mistakes?

The biggest mistake is transferring from inbox to outbox on every dequeue instead of only when the outbox is empty. This not only destroys the O(1) amortized bound but also breaks the FIFO ordering because newly enqueued elements get mixed into the transfer.

```java
package com.bank.core;

public class EagerTransferBug {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // If we transfer on every dequeue:
        // enqueue A, B, C
        // dequeue: transfer [A,B,C] to outbox -> pop A (correct so far)
        // enqueue D
        // dequeue: transfer [D] to outbox (which still has B,C) -> WRONG
        // The outbox already had older elements; adding D on top breaks order
        System.out.println("Eager transfer mixes new elements with old ones");
        System.out.println("Rule: transfer ONLY when outbox is empty");
    }
}
```

```text
Eager transfer mixes new elements with old ones
Rule: transfer ONLY when outbox is empty
```

### How is this applied in banking?

At the National Bank of India, a legacy cheque-clearing module exposed only stack-based buffers, but cheques must be processed in arrival order. The two-stack queue gave FIFO semantics over stack primitives. The lazy transfer kept throughput high because clearing happens in bursts: a batch of cheques arrives, fills the inbox, and a single transfer empties them in correct order.

```java
package com.bank.service;

public class ChequeClearingDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        QueueFromStacks<String> clearing = new QueueFromStacks<>();

        // Batch arrival
        clearing.enqueue("CHQ-001: Rajesh 50000");
        clearing.enqueue("CHQ-002: Priya 25000");
        clearing.enqueue("CHQ-003: Pankaj 75000");

        // Process in arrival order
        System.out.println("Clearing: " + clearing.dequeue());
        System.out.println("Clearing: " + clearing.dequeue());

        // New arrival while processing
        clearing.enqueue("CHQ-004: Rajesh 30000");

        System.out.println("Clearing: " + clearing.dequeue());
        System.out.println("Clearing: " + clearing.dequeue());
    }
}
```

```text
Clearing: CHQ-001: Rajesh 50000
Clearing: CHQ-002: Priya 25000
Clearing: CHQ-003: Pankaj 75000
Clearing: CHQ-004: Rajesh 30000
```

### What should a senior engineer know?

In normal Java, use `ArrayDeque` as a real queue. The two-stack construction earns its place when a stack is the only primitive available, such as implementing a queue over an immutable stack in functional code. It is the idea behind the Banker's queue in functional data structures, where two lists play the role of the two stacks. The key interview insight is explaining amortized analysis: worst-case O(n) for one call does not mean O(n) per operation on average.

```java
package com.bank.core;

public class SeniorNotes {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Real Java code: use ArrayDeque as a queue directly");
        System.out.println("Two-stack queue: useful when only stacks are available");
        System.out.println("Functional programming: Banker's queue = two lists");
        System.out.println("Interview key: explain amortized O(1) vs worst-case O(n)");
    }
}
```

```text
Real Java code: use ArrayDeque as a queue directly
Two-stack queue: useful when only stacks are available
Functional programming: Banker's queue = two lists
Interview key: explain amortized O(1) vs worst-case O(n)
```
